{"name":"Marshy Habitat","hex":"#b8aea2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"marshy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, or resembling a marsh; boggy.","Growing in marshy ground."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/marshy"},{"term":"habitat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Conditions suitable for an organism or population of organisms to live.","A range; a place where a species naturally occurs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/habitat"}],"entries":[{"title":"Marsh","blocks":[{"text":"In ecology, a marsh is a wetland that is dominated by herbaceous plants rather than by woody plants. More in general, the word can be used for any low-lying and seasonally waterlogged terrain. In Europe and in agricultural literature, low-lying meadows that require draining and embanked polderlands are also referred to as marshes or marshland."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Meadowlands_Lyndhurst.jpg/330px-Meadowlands_Lyndhurst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsh","wikidata":"Q30198"},{"title":"Habitat","blocks":[{"text":"In ecology, habitat refers to the array of resources, biotic factors that are present in an area, such as to support the survival and reproduction of a particular species. A species' habitat can be seen as the physical manifestation of its ecological niche. Thus \"habitat\" is a species-specific term, fundamentally different from concepts such as environment or vegetation assemblages, for which the term \"habitat-type\" is more appropriate."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Beech_Forest_%28AU%29%2C_Great_Otway_National_Park%2C_Beauchamp_Falls_--_2019_--_1271.jpg/330px-Beech_Forest_%28AU%29%2C_Great_Otway_National_Park%2C_Beauchamp_Falls_--_2019_--_1271.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"ht